<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Maysalun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-syrian-war">Franco-Syrian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Maysalun was fought on 24 July 1920 at the Maysalun pass west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> between French forces under General Mariano Goybet and a small Syrian army led by the war minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-azma">Yusuf al-Azma</a>. The French victory, in which al-Azma was killed, ended King Faisal's Arab Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and opened the way to French Mandate rule. It became a symbol of resistance in Syrian nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-maysalun.jpg" alt="Battle of Maysalun" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gouraud_inspecting_troops_at_Maysalun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Paris Peace Conference of 1919 Patriarch Elias Peter Hoayek won an enlarged territory for the Maronites, including Muslim and Druze districts, and after Faisal's Arab Kingdom of Syria fell at the <b>Battle of Maysalun</b> in 1920 the San Remo Conference placed Syria and Lebanon under French rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Faisal I's Kingdom of Syria of 1920 lasted months before the <b>Battle of Maysalun</b>, and France ruled under a League of Nations mandate from 1920 and crushed Sultan al-Atrash's revolt of 1925–1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France sent an ultimatum, and on 24 July 1920 its army defeated the Syrian force at <b>Maysalun</b>, where the war minister Yusuf al-Azma was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-kingdom-of-syria">Arab Kingdom of Syria</a></li><li><blockquote>French forces overthrew the Arab Kingdom of Syria at <b>Maysalun</b> that July, and the League of Nations formally confirmed the mandate in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon">French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the Arab Kingdom of Syria at <b>Maysalun</b> in July 1920, the French high commissioner Henri Gouraud divided Syria into several states to weaken Arab nationalism.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-damascus">State of Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
